Let me re-check this. The Boston Marathon was first run on Patriots' Day, April 19, **1897**, organized by the Boston Athletic Association. It was inspired by the 1896 Athens Olympics marathon, but the Boston race itself did not take place until 1897 — there was no 1896 running of the Boston Marathon. My earlier reasoning about a "fall 1896" inaugural race was fabricated; the BAA took until spring 1897 to organize the event. The 1897 winner was John J. McDermott. The marathon is celebrated as the world's oldest annual marathon precisely because it has been held every year since **1897**.

So my earlier answer (A) was incorrect.

